Lab 8: Multi-Model Deployment and A/B Testing
Objective
Deploy two versions of a deep learning model locally, and simulate traffic distribution between them to conduct A/B testing — mimicking the behavior of cloud load balancers without requiring any cloud services.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Ensure the following are installed:

Python 3.8+

Flask (pip install flask)

Nginx (sudo apt install nginx)

Steps
Step 1: Prepare Two Model Variants
Create a working directory:

mkdir -p ~/dl-ab-lab/{model_v1,model_v2}
cd ~/dl-ab-lab
Variant A (Model V1):
cd model_v1
cat > app.py <<EOF
from flask import Flask, request, jsonify
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/predict', methods=['POST'])
def predict():
    return jsonify({"model_version": "v1", "prediction": "class-A"})

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(port=5001)
EOF
Variant B (Model V2):
cd ../model_v2
cat > app.py <<EOF
from flask import Flask, request, jsonify
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/predict', methods=['POST'])
def predict():
    return jsonify({"model_version": "v2", "prediction": "class-B"})

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(port=5002)
EOF
Install Flask in each environment (or globally in virtualenv):
pip install flask
Run both models in separate terminals:
# Terminal 1
cd ~/dl-ab-lab/model_v1
python3 app.py

# Terminal 2
cd ~/dl-ab-lab/model_v2
python3 app.py
Step 2: Configure Nginx for A/B Traffic Split
Create a custom Nginx config:

sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/ab_testing
Paste the following configuration:

upstream ab_test {
    server 127.0.0.1:5001 weight=1;
    server 127.0.0.1:5002 weight=1;
}

server {
    listen 8080;

    location /predict {
        proxy_pass http://ab_test;
    }
}
Enable the config:

sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/ab_testing /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
sudo nginx -t
sudo systemctl restart nginx
Test A/B traffic routing:

curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/predict -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{}'
Repeat the above command a few times to see responses alternating between v1 and v2.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Deployed two separate versions of a model API using Flask

Used Nginx as a local load balancer to distribute traffic (A/B testing)

Verified that traffic was routed between model variants

This fully offline simulation helps you understand multi-model deployment strategies and A/B testing workflows, mirroring cloud load balancing behavior on your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine.
